  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Daric (Daric coin) was one of the most famous gold coins of the Achaemenid
    Persian Empire, introduced during the reign of Darius I (522\u2013486 BCE). It
    was widely used as a high-value currency throughout the empire and became an
    important symbol of Persian economic power. The coin was made of nearly pure
    gold and typically weighed around 8.4 grams. Its name is believed to be derived
    from Darius himself, making it closely associated with the authority and wealth
    of the Achaemenid kings.\nThe obverse of the Daric usually depicts the Persian
    Great King as a royal archer, holding a bow and spear, while the reverse bears
    an incuse punch mark rather than a detailed image. The archer design represented
    the military strength and royal authority of Persia. Darics were especially valuable
    for large payments, including military expenses and tribute, and their consistent
    weight and gold content helped them circulate across the vast Achaemenid Empire."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7308c1d742b3a46bbb0f12fa01af6bb5, type: 2}
