      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Cyrus Cylinder is an ancient clay cylinder inscribed in Babylonian
    cuneiform after the conquest of Babylon by Cyrus the Great, founder of the Achaemenid
    Persian Empire, in 539 BCE. The text presents Cyrus as a legitimate ruler chosen
    by the Babylonian god Marduk and describes his restoration of temples and religious
    practices in Babylon. It also portrays his rule as bringing order and prosperity
    to the region.\nThe cylinder is an important source for understanding Achaemenid
    imperial ideology and Babylonian political traditions. It is sometimes described
    as the \u201Cfirst declaration of human rights,\u201D but modern historians generally
    do not consider this an accurate description because the text is a royal inscription
    rather than a universal statement of human rights. Today, the Cyrus Cylinder
    is one of the best-known surviving artifacts associated with Cyrus the Great
    and the early Achaemenid Empire."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 85feb384871024497a0253421cabf9f3, type: 2}
