  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The Siglos (Siglos coin) was a silver coin used during the Achaemenid
    Persian Empire, particularly for everyday and military transactions. Its name
    comes from an ancient unit of weight, and a typical Siglos weighed around 5.5
    grams. Unlike the gold Daric, which was mainly used for large payments, the Siglos
    served as a more practical silver denomination and circulated widely throughout
    the Persian Empire.

    The obverse of the Siglos commonly shows the Persian
    Great King as a warrior, holding a bow and sometimes a spear or dagger, emphasizing
    the military authority of the Achaemenid monarchy. The reverse usually features
    a simple incuse punch mark, created when the coin was struck. Sigloi were especially
    important for paying soldiers and handling smaller-scale commerce, making them
    an essential part of the Achaemenid monetary system alongside the gold Daric.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7308c1d742b3a46bbb0f12fa01af6bb5, type: 2}
